# Baseline course-morning pools, group courses_hidden (docs/authoring/pools.md):
# the three hidden courses, Artifice, Starcalling and Hollow Studies. Repeatable routine events, two each at level I.

# ---------------- Artifice (track: artifice, skills alchemy arcana; Mira's Workshop) ----------------

@event art_escapement_drill
title: The Ticking Hour
track: artifice
level: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: gear
tags: class study
scene: A cluttered workshop bench covered in tiny brass gears, files and loupes, a row of half-built clockwork hearts ticking out of time with one another.
text: {Artifice begins with a heart, a fist of clockwork that must tick evenly before it is allowed to think.|Your clockwork heart is waiting where you left it, sulking at the wrong speed.|You can hear your own heart on the bench from the doorway now. Its tick is almost yours.} {~The bench beside you ticks like hail.|One heart near the door is running backwards, proudly.|The lecturer listens to each bench with eyes closed.} {season == "winter": The brass is cold enough to bite.|The workshop smells of solder.}
> left: File every tooth by hand
  alchemy +small
  mood -small
  say: {~Tooth by tooth, under a loupe, until your eyes water. The tick evens out by the bell.|You file all morning and burn one thumb on the solder. The heart ticks steady and slightly smug.|It's slow, fiddly work, and your hands learn it faster than your head does.}
> right: Charm the spring to keep time
  challenge enchant using arcana medium:
    win:
      arcana +small
      study artifice +tiny
      say: {~The charm settles into the spring like a cat into a basket. Your heart ticks like a metronome.|You whisper the rhythm into the coil and it keeps it. The lecturer opens one eye, then closes it again.}
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {~The spring takes the charm too literally and unwinds all at once, across the room.|Your heart ticks perfectly for a minute, then very fast, then not at all. Someone hands you a fire bucket just in case.}

@event art_stirring_cauldron
title: The Cauldron That Stirs
track: artifice
level: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cauldron
tags: class magic
scene: A copper cauldron on iron legs stirring itself with a long brass arm while students crowd round with notebooks and a wary distance.
text: {The class project is a cauldron that stirs itself. It does, mostly, in whatever direction it pleases.|The cauldron has been rebuilt again. It stirs with a new brass arm and the same old temper.|The cauldron recognises you, or behaves as if it does.} {~Today it stirs clockwise, then argues.|Today it stirs so fast the porridge in it is climbing out.|Today the arm keeps tapping the rim like an impatient guest.} {final_year: You'll miss its moods, a little.|The fire buckets by the door are full, for once.}
> left: Recut the runes on the arm
  study artifice +small
  health -tiny
  say: {~You spend the morning with a graver and a magnifier, recutting the stirring runes one stroke at a time. Your fingers are nicked; the arm moves sweetly.|Fine work in brass, the whole lesson long. The arm stirs evenly, and you have solder burns in a neat little row.}
> right: Test it on a live brew
  challenge brew medium:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      study artifice +tiny
      say: {~The cauldron stirs your brew exactly as the recipe asks, and you take the credit for both.|The brew turns the right colour at the right minute. The cauldron seems to know it did well.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {~The arm speeds up, the brew climbs the walls, and the whole class steps back as one.|The cauldron stirs your brew into something with the texture of an argument. You scrub it out until the bell.}

# ---------------- Starcalling (track: starcalling, skills lore arcana; the Observatory, at night) ----------------

@event star_names_in_the_dark
title: Names in the Dark
track: starcalling
level: 1
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: star
tags: class study
scene: A dark brass dome open to a sky full of stars, a student at the eyepiece of a great telescope while another recites from memory, breath steaming.
text: {Starcalling is taught by appointment, at night, and it begins with names. "You cannot call what you cannot name."|Another night appointment under the dome. The list of names is longer than it was.|The dome at night feels like yours now. You know where the draught comes in.} {~The great telescope has swung round on its own again.|A meteor scratches the sky and is gone.|An owl is sitting on the open shutter, listening.} {season == "winter": Your breath freezes on the eyepiece.|The night air smells of cut grass from the lawns below.}
> left: Recite the names until perfect
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {~You say them over and over into the cold until they stop being words and start being places.|Name after name, in order and then backwards. By the end you could find them blindfold, and nearly have to.|You stumble on the same name four times. The fifth time it comes out right, and you feel it land somewhere.}
> right: Let the telescope choose a star
  challenge research easy:
    win:
      study starcalling +small
      say: {~The telescope settles on a star you've never heard of. You find its name in the old charts before the lecturer can tell you.|Wherever it points, you follow, and the charts agree with it. That's a whole lesson learned the other way round.}
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {~The telescope picks a star and then changes its mind every ten minutes. You chase it round the dome until midnight.|You spend the night at the eyepiece and come down with a stiff neck and one name you aren't sure of.}

@event star_thread_of_light
title: A Thread of Light
track: starcalling
level: 1
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: sparkle
tags: class magic
scene: A student in a dark observatory with hands cupped upward, a single silver thread of starlight running down through the open dome into their palms.
text: {Tonight you try to call down a single thread of a named star's light, no thicker than a hair.|The lesson is the same as ever: one thread, one star, held as long as you can bear it.|You've called down threads before. Tonight the lecturer wants it held longer.} {~The sky is clear and very far away.|Thin cloud keeps crossing the star you've chosen.|The other students are already standing with their hands raised.}
> left: Call the thread down yourself
  study starcalling +small
  mana -small
  say: {~The thread comes down cold and bright and pours into your palms. You hold it until your hands shake.|It takes three tries. On the third, the light drops into your hands like water from a great height.|A hair of light, the length of the whole tower. When it fades, your fingers glow for an hour.}
> right: Keep the night log instead
  arcana +small
  say: {~You write down every thread the others call: the star, the minute, how long it held. It's dull, cold work, and you start to see the pattern.|You hold the lamp and the log while the others call. Watching from outside, you see exactly where their hands go wrong.} {season == "winter": Your ink keeps freezing.|The lecturer reads your log twice.}
> up[starlight]: Hold your own starlight up to it
  study starcalling +small
  say: {~You light your own starlight and the thread finds it, like calling to like. It holds twice as long.|Your starlight steadies the thread, and the lecturer has you show the others how.|The thread drops straight into the light you're already holding. For a moment the dome is bright as noon.}

# ---------------- Hollow Studies (track: hollow_studies, skills guile lore; the headmistress, a room without windows) ----------------

@event hollow_sealed_boxes
title: How It Was Sealed
track: hollow_studies
level: 1
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: lock
tags: class mystery
scene: A windowless room lined with black-bound books, a single lamp on a table, a tall calm woman setting a small locked box in front of a student.
text: {Each lesson, the headmistress sets a sealed thing in front of you. "Tell me how it was closed," she says. "Not what is inside. How."|Another sealed thing on the table. Tonight it is {~a letter with a wax seal|a small lacquered box|a jar with its lid charmed shut}.|The headmistress places tonight's sealed thing with a little smile. She has started choosing harder ones.} {~The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves breathe slowly in the dark.|She sits with her hands folded, unhurried.}
> left: Study the seal for the hour
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {~You trace every line of it without touching. At the end you can name each step of the sealing, in order. She nods once.|It takes the whole hour and a headache, but you work it out backwards, layer by layer.|You get two layers right and one wrong. She tells you which, kindly, and you'll remember.}
> right: Open it without leaving a trace
  challenge deceive medium:
    win:
      guile +small
      study hollow_studies +tiny
      say: {~You open it and close it again, and nothing shows. She looks at it, then at you, and laughs, short and delighted.|Open, closed, not a mark. "Good," she says. "Now never do that to a door."}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {~The seal cracks with a tiny, final sound. The headmistress writes something in her book. She remembers every mistake, and now there is one more.|It opens, and it shows. "Everyone breaks one," she says pleasantly. "Rarely so loudly."}

@event hollow_reading_the_blank
title: Reading the Blank Page
track: hollow_studies
level: 1
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: book
tags: class mystery
scene: A student bent over an apparently blank page by lamplight, faint lines of ink surfacing under their fingertips, black-bound books leaning in from the shelves.
text: {"Most things that are hidden are not gone," the headmistress says, and gives you a blank page. "Find what was written. Gently."|A new blank page tonight. It feels warmer than paper should.|You know the weight of hidden writing in a page by now, like a pocket with something in it.} {~The books on the nearest shelf seem to lean closer.|The lamp burns low and blue.|Somewhere in the stacks, a book turns its own page.} {final_year: She asks where you'll go after graduation, and listens.|She is reading while you work, and doesn't look up.}
> left: Coax the ink up slowly
  study hollow_studies +small
  mana -small
  say: {~You warm the page with a thread of power, a breath at a time. The writing surfaces slowly, safe and dull: someone's laundry list, very carefully hidden.|Line by line, the ink comes up. It's an old timetable. The lesson was in the finding.|It takes most of your strength. The page gives up a single sentence about the weather, decades ago.}
> right: Read it bare-handed
  guile +small
  health -tiny
  say: {~The ink comes up fast under your fingers, and so does a chill that stays in your hand until morning.|You read it quickly and well. Your fingertips ache for an hour, and the headmistress hands you a cup of tea without comment.}
> up[ward]: Let your ward do the reading
  lore +small
  say: {~Your ward settles over the page like a held breath, and the writing rises into it. The headmistress raises an eyebrow, pleased.|You read through the ward as if through glass. It costs you, but not your hands.|The hidden lines surface inside your shell, one by one, in no hurry.}
